| 255 | a comparison of two SCALARS is the host's `Boolean`, not APL's 0/1, so a dfn operand like `{⍺<⍵}` under `¨` or `∘.` hands a `Boolean` to an assemble-by-cell and the scalar contract refuses it | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (library) | silent | none | apl rung 6 | siblings: 218. `RR64`'s `opr <` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:180`) returns `Boolean` and the APL base declares no `(RR64, RR64)` comparison, so only scalar against scalar is not 0/1. The first run of `explorations/apl/rung-6/w02_lib.fss` died with a `CallerViolation` from `aplAsmV`'s `requires { aplAllScalar(rs) }` (`explorations/apl/base/AplCore.fss:1698` at c124c6b65). Workaround, in place: `aplIsScalar` accepts a `Boolean` and `aplScalarOf` maps it to 1.0 / 0.0 (`explorations/apl/base/AplCore.fss:1685-1688`); with it case (b) of `w02_lib.fss` and `Rung6.fss` `Z13` pass, with `explorations/apl/base` on the source path (`rung-6/PROBES.md`). full text: history, row 255 |
| 436 | under walk, an empty or filtered `SUM` over an additive group that is not a number type ends in `CastError`: the identity's fallback casts the numeral `0` to the group, not its `zero` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library gap vs spec (library) | `basic/expressions/reductions.tex`, "Summations and Other Reduction Expressions"; `basic/evaluation/reduction.tex`, "Reduction Variables"; `Specification-1.0-frozen/advanced-lib/algebraic-constraints.tex:794-797`, "Operators and Their Properties" | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXEmptyGroupSumRungF.fss` | climb batch 6 rung F | `SUM[\M7\][i <- 0#0] M7(i)` (a user `AdditiveGroup[\M7\]` with its `zero`) ends in `CastError`, and so does `SUM[\M5\][i <- 1#4, i > 2] M5(i)`; an unfiltered nonempty sum runs. `SumReduction.empty()` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:3447` at c124c6b65) calls `additiveIdentity`, whose `else => cast[\T\](0)` (`:3408`) casts a numeral; `multiplicativeIdentity`'s `else => cast[\T\](1)` (`:3425`) does so for `PROD`. Workaround: accumulate in a `seq` loop that starts from the group's `zero`. Repair: `HasIdentity` (P-78; `perf-probes/where-clauses/REPORT.md` B.4). full text: history, row 436 |
| 481 | on the compiled path a range's `=` is identity: `(1:3) = (1#3)` and `(3:1) = (5:4)` print `false` compiled and `true` under walk; a range equals only itself | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library bug (prelude) | `Specification/basic/expressions/ranges.tex`, "Ranges" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXRangeEqRungJ.fss` | climb batch 7R rung J's skeptic | siblings: 479. The compiler library's `GeneratorZZ32` extends `Equality[\GeneratorZZ32\]` (`Library/CompilerLibrary.fsi:111`, `.fss:301` at c124c6b65), whose `=` is `self === other` (`Library/CompilerAlgebra.fss:26`), and no range object overrides it. The test asserts `(1:3) = (1#3)`, `(3:1) = (5:4)` and `NOT ((2:4) = (2:5))`; walk passes all three, the compiled run stops at the first. That `r = r` is `true` compiled is measured but not gated. The compiler library takes no new declaration (POSITIONS, "The library route"): the one library's `=` replaces it at the switch-over. full text: history, row 481 |
| 603 | `SimpleMappedSeqIndexed`, the map of the full sequential ranges, is not a `MappedGenerator`, so its `reverse` is `Indexed`'s: `seq(0:6:2).map(fn x => x + 1).reverse.asString` prints `SimpleReversedIndexed(mapped(seq(1,3,5,7)))`, no longer `mapped(7,5,3,1)` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library bug (library) | silent | `ProjectFortress/tests/RangeDeclarations.fss` | climb batch 9 rung R's skeptic | The object (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4319-4320` at c124c6b65) lacks `MappedGenerator`'s `reverse`, `reduce`, `g` and `f` (`:3751-3762`): the api does not export `MappedGenerator`, and the export check compares extends clauses (`ExportChecker.scala:733-734`). Its parallel twin `SimpleMappedIndexed` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:3771-3772`) extends it and prints `mapped(6,4,2,0)` for `(0#4).map(f).reverse`. Elements and order are right; the test pins today's string (`ProjectFortress/tests/RangeDeclarations.fss:58`). Workaround: compare elements. Repair: export and extend `MappedGenerator`, or declare `reverse` as `SimpleMappedIndexed[\E,F,I\](g0.reverse, f0)`. full text: history, row 603 |
| 609 | an implicit strided range `:b:c` used as a subscript is completed by counting back from `b` in steps of `c`, not as `l:b:c`: under walk `<\|a,b,c,d,e,f\|>[:3:2]` is `<\|b, d\|>` and `"abcdef"[:3:2]` is `bd` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library bug (library) | `Specification/basic/expressions/ranges.tex`, "Ranges" (the implicit range `:b:c` is treated as `l:b:c`) | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXSubscriptImplicitStridedRange.fss` | climb batch 9 rung S's skeptic | `RightScalarRange`'s `opr IN` tests `stride DIVIDES (r-n)` and `atMost` builds `r-(n-1)str` up to `r` (`Library/RangeInternals.fss:873-877` at c124c6b65), so `(0#6).narrowToRange(:3:2)` is `[1,3]` where `0:3:2`, the indices 0 and 2, is meant; an array's `[:3:2]` gives its elements 1 and 3. String inherits the completion through `self.bounds.narrowToRange(r0)` in its `opr[r0]` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4383`). Workaround: write the left end, `[0:3:2]`. Repair, in `RangeInternals`: complete `:b:c` from the left end of the axis it subscripts. full text: history, row 609 |
| 659 | a bounded range of rank 2 or 3 strided backwards on some axes and forwards on the others has no range kind, so `((0,0)#).every(-1,1)` stops walk | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (library) | silent | none | climb batch 11 rung L | `combine2D(i:BoundedScalarRange, j:BoundedScalarRange)` (`Library/RangeInternals.fss:639-640` at c124c6b65) fails "shouldn't happen: combine2D of non-uniform ranges ...4,3,2,1,0] and [0,1,2,3,4..." for a right and a left scalar range, as `combine2D(i:ScalarRange, j:ScalarRange)` (`:207-208`) does: no range kind of rank 2 or 3 mixes stride signs. Command: `bin/fortress` on a program that prints `(((0,0)#).every(-1,1)).asDebugString`; output: that message. The file is unchanged since abe8b0342, where this was run. Workaround: stride every axis forwards and read a backward axis's index as `n - 1 - i`. |
